The problem asks us to simulate a process involving $N$ aliens.
Initially, the $i$-th alien has $A_i$ stones.
The process runs for $N$ years.
At the end of year $i$ (where $i$ goes from 1 to $N$), the $i$-th alien becomes an adult.
When an alien becomes an adult, every *adult* who currently has at least one stone gives exactly one stone to the new adult.
We need to find the number of stones each alien has after $N$ years.

Let's denote the number of stones alien $i$ has at time $t$ as $C_i(t)$.
Initially, at $t=0$, $C_i(0) = A_i$.
The events happen at integer years $t=1, 2, \ldots, N$.
At year $t$, alien $t$ becomes an adult.
The condition "every adult who has at least one stone" applies to aliens who have already become adults (i.e., aliens $1, \ldots, t-1$) AND aliens who have already received stones and are still adults. Wait, the problem says "every adult who has at least one stone".
Alien $k$ becomes an adult at year $k$. So at year $t$, aliens $1, \ldots, t-1$ are adults. Alien $t$ is becoming an adult. Aliens $t+1, \ldots, N$ are minors.
So, when alien $t$ becomes an adult, the recipients of the gift are aliens $1, \ldots, t-1$ who currently have $\ge 1$ stone.
Alien $t$ receives one stone from each such adult.
Alien $t$ does not give stones to itself (it's the one becoming adult).
Aliens $t+1, \ldots, N$ are minors, so they don't give stones.

Let's trace the sample 1 to understand the rule better.
$N=4$. $A = [5, 0, 9, 3]$.
Initially: $C = [5, 0, 9, 3]$.

Year 1: Alien 1 becomes adult.
Adults before this: None.
Alien 1 receives 0 stones.
$C$ remains $[5, 0, 9, 3]$.
Adults set: $\{1\}$.

Year 2: Alien 2 becomes adult.
Adults before this: $\{1\}$.
Check Alien 1: $C_1 = 5 \ge 1$. Alien 1 gives 1 stone to Alien 2.
Alien 2 receives 1 stone.
$C_2$ becomes $0 + 1 = 1$.
$C_1$ remains 5.
$C$ becomes $[5, 1, 9, 3]$.
Adults set: $\{1, 2\}$.

Year 3: Alien 3 becomes adult.
Adults before this: $\{1, 2\}$.
Check Alien 1: $C_1 = 5 \ge 1$. Alien 1 gives 1 stone to Alien 3.
Check Alien 2: $C_2 = 1 \ge 1$. Alien 2 gives 1 stone to Alien 3.
Alien 3 receives $1 + 1 = 2$ stones.
$C_3$ becomes $9 + 2 = 11$.
$C$ becomes $[5, 1, 11, 3]$.
Adults set: $\{1, 2, 3\}$.

Year 4: Alien 4 becomes adult.
Adults before this: $\{1, 2, 3\}$.
Check Alien 1: $C_1 = 5 \ge 1$. Alien 1 gives 1 stone to Alien 4.
Check Alien 2: $C_2 = 1 \ge 1$. Alien 2 gives 1 stone to Alien 4.
Check Alien 3: $C_3 = 11 \ge 1$. Alien 3 gives 1 stone to Alien 4.
Alien 4 receives $1 + 1 + 1 = 3$ stones.
$C_4$ becomes $3 + 3 = 6$.
$C$ becomes $[5, 1, 11, 6]$.

